  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z}{4},\\,x=1\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\u27e80,2,3\u27e9.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\u27e80,-5,4\u27e9.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=\u27e80,-5,4\u27e9.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e80,-5,4\u27e9 & =\u27e80,2,3\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,-5,4\u27e9 \\\\ r\\cdot \u27e80,-5,4\u27e9 & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80,-5,4\u27e9 & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z-2 & =0 \\\\ 5y-4z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
